# Line endings, settled once.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which
# converts on checkout and back on commit. That mostly works and fails in one
# visible way: a file rewritten by a tool (gofmt -w, a formatter, a script)
# lands with endings that disagree with what git last stamped, so `git status`
# reports it as modified while `git diff` shows nothing at all. Nine files were
# sitting in exactly that state during the last audit — no content change,
# reported as changed.
#
# Declaring the intent here takes the decision away from each machine's
# configuration. Text is normalised to LF in the repository; what appears in a
# working tree is then consistent regardless of who cloned it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.msi binary

# Generated lockfiles are text but never worth reviewing as a diff.
package-lock.json -diff
Cargo.lock -diff
